@r2r0 I think that this would work indeed. Not need to add a supplemenary polling as it is already present with the following check in function `shutdown_ble_stack`.
```
while (LL_PWR_IsActiveFlag_C2DS() == 0) {
```
So the new code would looks like:
```
static void send_stack_reset(void)
{
	int err = 0;

	err = bt_hci_cmd_send(ACI_HAL_STACK_RESET, NULL);

	if (err) {
		LOG_ERR("M0 BLE stack reset issue");
	}
}
```

review_comments:
You can't assume that drivers implement a `close()` callback. This is important for backwards compatibility, and also if a `close()` callback doesn't make sense for some drivers (possibly most of them). So you should probably have a check right after the `if (!bt_dev.drv)` check:
```c
if (!bt_dev.drv->close) {
	return -ENOTSUP;
}
```

@erwango btw, I noticed that `drivers/bluetooth/hci/spi.c` also has these defines:https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/3cbfc505b357f80b6aca4cd2dc39c0f60a24c1b1/drivers/bluetooth/hci/spi.c#L106-L109

Perhaps they should be moved into a common header file?

It also seems like the defines here in `ipm_stm32wb.c` have slightly different values - I think the `0xFC..` part of the OCF is redundant, since that'll come from the OGF value when combined with it.
@jhedberg BLUENRG and STM32WB are different pieces, provided by different ST divisions. They might share some deep common ground, but that's anecdotal and not documented. So I prefer to keep them separated.
Regarding the redundant 0xFC, I can fix this in a follow up PR once this one is merged.
